  "task_f_remediation": {
    "discrepancy_root_cause": "The data-generating equation p_{3,gi} = clip(p_{1,gi} + delta_g + eps_{3,gi}, 0.01, 0.99) applies a lower-boundary clamp at 0.01. Because US English has the lowest baseline WER (14.2% -> 10.7% in Arm 3), 9.03% of its probability distribution fell below 0.01 and was clamped, shifting its expected Arm 3 error rate up by +0.31 pp. In contrast, South Asian English (23.5% -> 20.0%) had only 0.44% probability clamped at 0.01, shifting its error rate up by only +0.01 pp. Consequently, the population disparity range Range(WER_3) was compressed by -0.247 pp relative to Range(WER_1). When Task 6 specified target_disparity_effect_pp = 0.00 pp, the data-generating model actually imposed a true population disparity reduction of -0.25 pp. Event D passed 2.3% - 2.6% of the time because the data was drawn from an advantageous non-null distribution.",
    "remediation_applied": "1. Created derive_calibrated_five_stratum_treatment_effects() in candidate power simulator which solves for the exact accent-specific shifts delta_g that cancel boundary clipping asymmetry, ensuring true population Delta D = 0.00000 pp exactly.\n2. Verified Event D Type I error control under true Delta D = 0.0000 pp: empirical false-pass rate is strictly <= 0.625% (0.43% at N_g=220, 0.48% at N_g=285).\n3. Under harm scenarios (+0.5 pp and +1.0 pp), Event D false-pass rate is 0.000%, demonstrating conservative behavior.",
